steps.body:
`require` loads the package, and the braces pick out the classes you need, the same object destructuring you use on any object:

```javascript
const { MinPriorityQueue, MaxPriorityQueue } = require("@datastructures-js/priority-queue");
const low = new MinPriorityQueue();
low.enqueue(5);
low.enqueue(1);
low.enqueue(3);
console.log(low.front()); // 1, the smallest, still in the heap
console.log(low.dequeue()); // 1, now removed
console.log(low.size(), low.isEmpty()); // 2 false
const high = MaxPriorityQueue.fromArray([4, 9, 2]);
console.log(high.dequeue()); // 9
```

`enqueue` and `dequeue` cost O(log n), and `front`, `size`, and `isEmpty` cost O(1). `fromArray` builds a heap from a whole array at once in O(n), but it reorders the array you pass and keeps using it, so pass a copy (`[...arr]`) when you still need the original.

A heap is not sorted inside. It only promises that `front()` is the smallest (or the largest), so taking items out one `dequeue` at a time is how you get them in order.

**The gotcha:** `front()` and `dequeue()` on an empty heap return `null` instead of throwing, and `null` quietly counts as 0 in math. Check `isEmpty()` or `size()` before you take.

example.code:
const { MaxPriorityQueue } = require("@datastructures-js/priority-queue");
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const [kLine, pilesLine] = input.trim().split("\n");
const k = Number(kLine);
const heap = MaxPriorityQueue.fromArray(pilesLine.trim().split(/\s+/).map(Number));

for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) {
  const pile = heap.dequeue();
  const rest = pile - Math.floor(pile / 2);
  console.log(`took ${pile}, put back ${rest}`);
  heap.enqueue(rest);
}

let total = 0;
while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
  total += heap.dequeue();
}
console.log(`stones left ${total}`);

