steps.body:
To order objects, pass the constructor a function that returns the number to order by:

```javascript
const { MinPriorityQueue } = require("@datastructures-js/priority-queue");
const jobs = new MinPriorityQueue((job) => job.minutes);
jobs.enqueue({ name: "email", minutes: 5 });
jobs.enqueue({ name: "report", minutes: 30 });
jobs.enqueue({ name: "call", minutes: 15 });
console.log(jobs.dequeue().name); // email
```

`MaxPriorityQueue` takes the same kind of function. When ties need a rule, `PriorityQueue` takes a compare function that works like `sort`'s: a negative result means `a` comes out first. Chain the tie-break with `||`:

```javascript harness
const { MinPriorityQueue, MaxPriorityQueue, PriorityQueue } = require("@datastructures-js/priority-queue");
{{code}}
while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
  console.log(queue.dequeue().join(" "));
}
```

```javascript
const queue = new PriorityQueue((a, b) => b[1] - a[1] || a[0].localeCompare(b[0]));
queue.enqueue(["ivy", 2]);
queue.enqueue(["al", 3]);
queue.enqueue(["bo", 2]);
```

That queue gives `al 3`, then `bo 2`, then `ivy 2`: the highest number first, and names A to Z on a tie.

**The gotcha:** a heap is not stable. Two items that compare as equal can come out in either order. When arrival order matters, store an arrival number in each item and use it as the last tie-break.

example.code:
const { PriorityQueue } = require("@datastructures-js/priority-queue");
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const lines = input.trim().split("\n");

const waiting = new PriorityQueue((a, b) => b.severity - a.severity || a.arrived - b.arrived);
lines.forEach((line, i) => {
  const [name, severity] = line.trim().split(/\s+/);
  waiting.enqueue({ name, severity: Number(severity), arrived: i });
});

let turn = 1;
while (!waiting.isEmpty()) {
  const p = waiting.dequeue();
  console.log(`${turn}. ${p.name} (${p.severity})`);
  turn++;
}

