steps.body:
To find the k largest of many items, you don't need to sort them all. Keep a **min**-heap of at most k items: add each new item, and when the size passes k, dequeue the smallest. What stays are the k largest seen so far, and `front()` is the kth largest. Each step costs O(log k), so a scan of n items costs O(n log k).

```javascript
const { MinPriorityQueue } = require("@datastructures-js/priority-queue");
const top = new MinPriorityQueue();
for (const x of [5, 1, 9, 3, 7, 8]) {
  top.enqueue(x);
  if (top.size() > 3) {
    top.dequeue();
  }
}
console.log(top.front()); // 7, the 3rd largest
console.log(top.toArray()); // [ 7, 8, 9 ]
```

A min-heap for the largest looks backwards, but its top is the weakest of the items you keep, the one to drop when a better one arrives. `toArray()` returns a sorted copy and leaves the heap full. For the k smallest, flip it and keep a max-heap.

For pairs, the compare function must put the weakest on top too. To keep the k most common words, where a tie goes to the word earlier in the alphabet, the weakest is the lowest count and, on a tie, the later word: `(a, b) => a[1] - b[1] || b[0].localeCompare(a[0])`.

**The gotcha:** the heap empties weakest first, so `dequeue` in a loop gives the k items from worst to best. Collect them, then `reverse()`.

example.code:
const { PriorityQueue } = require("@datastructures-js/priority-queue");
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const [kLine, ...lines] = input.trim().split("\n");
const k = Number(kLine);

// the weakest on top: the lower score, and on a tie the later name
const top = new PriorityQueue((a, b) => a[1] - b[1] || b[0].localeCompare(a[0]));
for (const line of lines) {
  const [name, score] = line.trim().split(/\s+/);
  top.enqueue([name, Number(score)]);
  if (top.size() > k) {
    top.dequeue();
  }
}

const best = [];
while (!top.isEmpty()) {
  best.push(top.dequeue());
}
best.reverse();
for (const [name, score] of best) {
  console.log(`${name} ${score}`);
}

